Alright, the last edit I made to studio.js was tweaking the default camera position and angles to give a better view in the studio editor. The tests ran and the screenshots look good, but I still notice that the horizon grid in the 3D studio view still has that moiré smear when the camera rotates, especially on higher‑resolution displays. The previous comment in the code even mentions that “fewer divisions + transparency kills the moiré smear at the horizon,” but the current parameters (grid size 240, divisions 120, opacity 0.45) are still a bit heavy and the lines are too bright, making the smear more noticeable.

I recall from earlier design discussions that we wanted a cleaner, more subtle grid that doesn’t dominate the scene. The plan was to reduce the grid size and divisions, adjust the colors to be a bit darker, lower the opacity, and even enable fog on the grid material so that lines fade out toward the horizon. This should eliminate the visual artifact and match the newer aesthetic we applied elsewhere (the hero blocks, auth background, etc.).

Looking at the existing block of code:

```js
    // fewer divisions + transparency kills the moiré smear at the horizon
    this.grid = new THREE.GridHelper(240, 120, 0x2a4a5a, 0x18242c);
    this.grid.position.y = 0.01;
    this.grid.material.transparent = true;
    this.grid.material.opacity = 0.45;
    this.grid.material.depthWrite = false;
    scene.add(this.grid);
```

I need to replace it with a version that reflects the updated design goals. The new parameters should be:

- Size 140 instead of 240 (smaller overall grid).
- Divisions 70 instead of 120 (fewer lines, less chance of aliasing).
- Primary color 0x3a607a (a slightly lighter, more muted teal) and secondary color 0x1c2c38 (darker background).
- Keep the tiny Y offset.
- Keep transparency and depthWrite false.
- Lower opacity to 0.3 to make it less intrusive.
- Add `this.grid.material.fog = true;` so the grid respects scene fog and fades out.

Thus the replacement string will be:

```js
    // compact + translucent + fogged so lines never smear at the horizon
    this.grid = new THREE.GridHelper(140, 70, 0x3a607a, 0x1c2c38);
    this.grid.position.y = 0.01;
    this.grid.material.transparent = true;
    this.grid.material.opacity = 0.3;
    this.grid.material.depthWrite = false;
    this.grid.material.fog = true;
    scene.add(this.grid);
```

I’ll use the Edit tool with `replace_all: false` because I only want to replace this specific block, not every occurrence. The `old_string` is the exact current block (including comments and spacing) and the `new_string` is the updated block I just composed. This should compile fine (the syntax matches the original) and will improve the visual quality of the studio grid without affecting other functionality. Proceed with the edit.

